--TEST--
`Core\Uri::tryParse` answers `null` at exactly the subjects `Core\Uri::parse` throws at and the same URI everywhere else, over a sweep of both of `parse`'s refusing steps — spec § 12, ADR 0066 § 3a
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `tryParse` is `parse` with `null` where it throws, and the point of writing
// it that way is that a validator written as separate code from the parser is
// how `filter_var(FILTER_VALIDATE_URL)` came to accept user-info `parse_url`
// read differently. So what is asserted here is not what either member
// answered on any one line — it is that the two **agree**, counted, over every
// subject the sweep can build.
array<string> $subjects = [
    // Read: the seven components together, then each shape RFC 3986 § 4.1
    // admits on its own. An empty port is § 3.2.3's own "absent", not a
    // refusal, and both ends of the TCP range are inside the bound.
    "https://user:pw@example.com:8080/a/b?q=1#f",
    "http://h/",
    "http://[::1]:80/",
    "http://h:/",
    "http://h:0/",
    "http://h:65535/",
    "mailto:a@b",
    "//h/p",
    "/p",
    "p",
    "a%2Fb",
    "?q",
    "#f",
    "",
    // Refused, and by **both** of `parse`'s throwing steps rather than one:
    // the first two are a port the grammar admits and § 3.2.3's prose does not
    // (`65536` is one past the bound `65535` above), the rest are the three
    // kinds of text the grammar itself refuses — a `%` that begins no escape,
    // a bracketed host that is no IPv6 address, and a byte outside the
    // grammar. A member folding only the grammar step passes every later line
    // and fails the count.
    "http://h:65536/",
    "http://h:99999/",
    "%zz",
    "%",
    "http://[not-v6]/",
    "http://a b/",
    "ht<tp://h/",
];

int $agreed = 0;
int $refused = 0;
int $read = 0;

foreach ($subjects as string $subject) {
    string $p = "";
    try {
        $p = Core\Uri::parse($subject)->toString();
    } catch (Throwable $threw) {
        $p = "<throw>";
    }

    // `tryParse` is caught too, so a subject it starts throwing at is a
    // disagreement on the count rather than an aborted case.
    string $t = "";
    try {
        ?Core\Uri $u = Core\Uri::tryParse($subject);
        if ($u == null) {
            $t = "<null>";
        } else {
            $t = $u->toString();
        }
    } catch (Throwable $tried) {
        $t = "<throw>";
    }

    // Where `parse` threw, agreement is `null`. Where it read, agreement is
    // the same URI: `parse` reports rather than normalizes, so `toString`
    // hands the subject's own text back and the two are comparable as text.
    if ($p == "<throw>") {
        $refused = $refused + 1;
        if ($t == "<null>") { $agreed = $agreed + 1; }
    } else {
        $read = $read + 1;
        if ($p == $t) { $agreed = $agreed + 1; }
    }

    echo "[", $subject, "] parse=", $p, " tryParse=", $t, "\n";
}

echo "read=", $read, " refused=", $refused, " agreed=", $agreed, "/", Core\Arr::count($subjects) as int, "\n";
--EXPECT--
[https://user:pw@example.com:8080/a/b?q=1#f] parse=https://user:pw@example.com:8080/a/b?q=1#f tryParse=https://user:pw@example.com:8080/a/b?q=1#f
[http://h/] parse=http://h/ tryParse=http://h/
[http://[::1]:80/] parse=http://[::1]:80/ tryParse=http://[::1]:80/
[http://h:/] parse=http://h:/ tryParse=http://h:/
[http://h:0/] parse=http://h:0/ tryParse=http://h:0/
[http://h:65535/] parse=http://h:65535/ tryParse=http://h:65535/
[mailto:a@b] parse=mailto:a@b tryParse=mailto:a@b
[//h/p] parse=//h/p tryParse=//h/p
[/p] parse=/p tryParse=/p
[p] parse=p tryParse=p
[a%2Fb] parse=a%2Fb tryParse=a%2Fb
[?q] parse=?q tryParse=?q
[#f] parse=#f tryParse=#f
[] parse= tryParse=
[http://h:65536/] parse=<throw> tryParse=<null>
[http://h:99999/] parse=<throw> tryParse=<null>
[%zz] parse=<throw> tryParse=<null>
[%] parse=<throw> tryParse=<null>
[http://[not-v6]/] parse=<throw> tryParse=<null>
[http://a b/] parse=<throw> tryParse=<null>
[ht<tp://h/] parse=<throw> tryParse=<null>
read=14 refused=7 agreed=21/21
